33
00:01:24,981 --> 00:01:26,948
-I really think this
is going to be it.

34
00:01:26,983 --> 00:01:29,399
This is my year.

35
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:32,161
-You say that every year we goto the Mad Scientist convention.

36
00:01:32,195 --> 00:01:34,301
-Ah, but this year is different.

37
00:01:34,335 --> 00:01:36,924
They laughed when I made
the more painful mousetrap,

38
00:01:36,958 --> 00:01:39,961
but my entrance into
the Mad Scientist competition

39
00:01:39,996 --> 00:01:41,653
is going to make me
famous.

40
00:01:41,687 --> 00:01:42,516
-Infamous.

41
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:43,965
-Ah, that too, that too.

42
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,036
-Dude, promise me if you
lose the contest this year

43
00:01:46,071 --> 00:01:48,867
you're not gonna blow up the
whole convention center again.

44
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:51,697
-I only did that once.
-Oh. Huh.

45
00:01:51,732 --> 00:01:52,940
-Okay. Twice.

46
00:01:52,974 --> 00:01:54,700
Twice, it was --
-Three times.

47
00:01:54,735 --> 00:01:57,462
-The third time
I use the incendiaries

48
00:01:57,496 --> 00:01:59,567
and it didn't actually make
the building blow up.

49
00:01:59,602 --> 00:02:02,950
It just made it
burn really quickly.

50
00:02:02,984 --> 00:02:05,055
God, that was beautiful,
wasn't it?

51
00:02:05,090 --> 00:02:06,333
-Okay, I'll give you that.

52
00:02:06,367 --> 00:02:08,162
Oh, i-it's time
to call Joel now.

53
00:02:08,197 --> 00:02:09,232
The experiment has to start.

54
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:10,889
-Right. Right. I know that.

55
00:02:10,923 --> 00:02:15,169
Come in, Jolene, you
free-floating space ferret.

56
00:02:15,204 --> 00:02:19,311
-While, sirs, I'm ready for
this week's invention exchange.

57
00:02:19,346 --> 00:02:22,176
You know how they have airbags
for cars but they haven't

58
00:02:22,211 --> 00:02:25,352
invented anything yet
for us motorcyclists.

59
00:02:25,386 --> 00:02:28,734
So I came up with this --
the airbag helmet.

60
00:02:28,769 --> 00:02:31,392
What do you think?
-Oh, great.

61
00:02:31,427 --> 00:02:35,327
Get Ralph Nader on the phone
and then call Gary Busey.

62
00:02:35,362 --> 00:02:36,915
-Is it our turn now?
-Yes.

63
00:02:36,949 --> 00:02:39,538
-Okay. This is our invention
for this week.

64
00:02:39,573 --> 00:02:41,161
It's called The Chalk Man.

65
00:02:41,195 --> 00:02:42,748
-Yes. We're entering this
in the mad scientist

66
00:02:42,783 --> 00:02:43,749
competition this year.

67
00:02:43,784 --> 00:02:44,854
-We have other choices.

68
00:02:44,888 --> 00:02:46,096
But this is the one
we're going with.

69
00:02:46,131 --> 00:02:48,202
-This is very strong.
-All right.

70
00:02:48,237 --> 00:02:50,204
Okay. You're throwing a party.

71
00:02:50,239 --> 00:02:52,793
It's 3:00 a.m. and none of
the guests are leaving.

72
00:02:52,827 --> 00:02:54,312
That's when you bring out
the Chalk Man.

73
00:02:54,346 --> 00:02:57,211
-Yes, as every self respecting
scientists knows,

74
00:02:57,246 --> 00:02:59,627
the sound of human fingernails
on a chalkboard

75
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:01,181
is most annoying.

76
00:03:01,215 --> 00:03:03,838
It initiates
the primal fear response

77
00:03:03,873 --> 00:03:06,634
in all mammals,
much like a chimpanzee

78
00:03:06,669 --> 00:03:09,499
scurrying across the plains
of the Serengeti in fear.

79
00:03:09,534 --> 00:03:12,088
Simply put your Chalk Man
on the platten.

80
00:03:12,122 --> 00:03:13,503
Now, this is a real chalkboard.

81
00:03:13,538 --> 00:03:15,954
-Put it on side "B".
-Side "B". Excellent.

82
00:03:15,988 --> 00:03:19,440
And just you'll notice
the tone arm

83
00:03:19,475 --> 00:03:22,788
has real human fingernails
embedded in the hand.

84
00:03:22,823 --> 00:03:24,790
Now place it on the platten.

85
00:03:24,825 --> 00:03:27,483
[Screeching]

86
00:03:27,517 --> 00:03:29,209
Ugh. Open it.

87
00:03:29,243 --> 00:03:31,107
-They'll stay.

88
00:03:31,141 --> 00:03:32,729
-Close it.
-They leave.

89
00:03:32,764 --> 00:03:34,490
[Screeching continues]

90
00:03:34,524 --> 00:03:36,492
-Open it.
-They'll stay.

91
00:03:36,526 --> 00:03:38,839
-Close it.
-They'll leave.

92
00:03:38,873 --> 00:03:40,806
-Open it.
-Stay.

93
00:03:40,841 --> 00:03:43,499
-And so on and so on
and so on.

94
00:03:43,533 --> 00:03:45,880
Well, what do you think
of that Joel-meister?

95
00:03:45,915 --> 00:03:47,606
-Wow, it's really annoying.

96
00:03:47,641 --> 00:03:49,125
-Thank you.

97
00:03:49,159 --> 00:03:51,886
-All right. So, uh, what's
this week's experiment?

98
00:03:51,921 --> 00:03:53,923
-It's called "The Robot
vs. The Aztec Mummy"

99
00:03:53,957 --> 00:03:56,408
and it's almost as annoying
as this Chalk Man here.

100
00:03:56,443 --> 00:03:57,547
[Laughing]
-Yes, but first

101
00:03:57,582 --> 00:03:58,928
we're going
to scour your palate

102
00:03:58,962 --> 00:04:01,758
with a little cinematic
science fiction sorbet.

103
00:04:01,793 --> 00:04:03,139
It's an old serial called

104
00:04:03,173 --> 00:04:06,246
"Commando Cody and the Rocket
Men From the Moon."

105
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:09,801
Uh, be careful, Larry, I've
installed a new security system

106
00:04:09,835 --> 00:04:11,803
here in Deep 13,
so, uh, be careful

107
00:04:11,837 --> 00:04:13,149
which buttons you push
on the panel. All right?

108
00:04:13,183 --> 00:04:15,255
-Oh, neato.
Okay, how does it work?

109
00:04:15,289 --> 00:04:17,740
-Oh, well, uh, stand over there
on that mark

110
00:04:17,774 --> 00:04:19,638
and, uh, pretend you're
from accounting.

111
00:04:19,673 --> 00:04:22,158
-Okay.

112
00:04:22,192 --> 00:04:24,056
You mean this mark here?
-Yes.

113
00:04:24,091 --> 00:04:26,473
-Oh!

114
00:04:26,507 --> 00:04:28,302
-Hmm, interesting.

115
00:04:28,337 --> 00:04:29,441
-[Groans]

116
00:04:32,375 --> 00:04:35,482
H-Here's your movie, Joel.

117
00:04:35,516 --> 00:04:38,692
[Alarms blaring]
-Movie Sign!

118
00:04:38,726 --> 00:04:44,249
[Metallic clanking]

119
00:04:44,284 --> 00:04:45,457
[Screeching]

120
00:04:47,425 --> 00:04:51,912
♪♪

121
00:04:51,946 --> 00:04:53,741
Aw, you're getting too heavy.

122
00:04:53,776 --> 00:04:56,054
-"A Republic Serial."

123
00:04:56,088 --> 00:04:58,505
-Yeah, you ever heard
of serials before?

124
00:04:58,539 --> 00:05:01,611
-Yeah. "Lights Out."
Oh, look it's pumpkin boy.

125
00:05:01,646 --> 00:05:05,235
-No, it's not that kind of
serial, so it means a, uh --

126
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:06,444
-Oh, so it's a wheat thing?

127
00:05:06,478 --> 00:05:08,756
-No, a movie that's in series.

128
00:05:08,791 --> 00:05:10,655
-What?

129
00:05:10,689 --> 00:05:13,968
-Oh, you'll catch on.
It's not a whole movie.

130
00:05:14,003 --> 00:05:16,764
-Why would someone go
and see part of a movie?

131
00:05:17,696 --> 00:05:19,940
-Uh...
I don't know.

132
00:05:19,974 --> 00:05:23,046
-There's always a boring short.
-Yeah.

133
00:05:23,081 --> 00:05:26,533
-My shorts are never boring.
-Thank you, Tom.

134
00:05:26,567 --> 00:05:31,745
♪♪

135
00:05:31,779 --> 00:05:34,541
-Is the music always
this poor in serials?

136
00:05:34,575 --> 00:05:38,614
-[Laughs] Yes.

137
00:05:38,648 --> 00:05:40,719
-I think I may get it, then.

138
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:45,897
-He's the heavy.

139
00:05:45,931 --> 00:05:48,555
Can tell when the music
swells up.

140
00:05:48,589 --> 00:05:49,728
-All right.

141
00:05:49,763 --> 00:05:58,427
♪♪

142
00:05:58,461 --> 00:06:01,706
I guess that's the end
of Chapter One.

143
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:06,469
Oklahoma. 1934.
The oil fields are ablaze.

144
00:06:06,504 --> 00:06:16,479
♪♪

145
00:06:16,514 --> 00:06:19,655
Holy.

146
00:06:19,689 --> 00:06:21,450
-Hey, look, there's a sale
at Penney's.

147
00:06:21,484 --> 00:06:23,452
-Big step, blowing up
everywhere.

148
00:06:23,486 --> 00:06:25,143
-Maybe it's a fire sale.

149
00:06:25,177 --> 00:06:27,041
-[Laughs]

150
00:06:27,076 --> 00:06:32,322
♪♪

151
00:06:32,357 --> 00:06:36,499
-New petition against tax law.

152
00:06:36,534 --> 00:06:38,984
-"A continued wave of 
death-dealing explosions

153
00:06:39,019 --> 00:06:41,608
struck yesterday 
at Area Defense Headquarters

154
00:06:41,642 --> 00:06:43,817
as in previous occasions, 
the authorities are unable

155
00:06:43,851 --> 00:06:45,439
to detect the cause 
of these blasts

156
00:06:45,474 --> 00:06:47,441
which have done untold damage."-Who's that guy mixing drinks?

157
00:06:47,476 --> 00:06:49,270
-Uh, Professor Tom Collins.

158
00:06:49,305 --> 00:06:50,996
-There are several theories, 
Joan,

159
00:06:51,031 --> 00:06:53,447
but so far no one is even sure 
what kind of explosives

160
00:06:53,482 --> 00:06:55,691
are being used or how 
they're set off.

161
00:06:55,725 --> 00:06:57,762
Maybe Mr. Henderson will have 
some information for us

162
00:06:57,796 --> 00:06:59,280
when he gets in 
from Washington.

163
00:06:59,315 --> 00:07:01,800
-That guy Henderson wouldn't 
tell you what time it is.

164
00:07:01,835 --> 00:07:04,216
Just what is his job anyway?
-Maybe he doesn't have a watch.

165
00:07:04,251 --> 00:07:05,701
-He really doesn't have any 
idea but it's a big one.

166
00:07:05,735 --> 00:07:08,393
He only answers to a few people in this country of ours.

167
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:09,774
I think that -- 
[Knock on door]

168
00:07:09,808 --> 00:07:13,225
-And they're all named Sue.

169
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:14,848
-Hello, Mr. Henderson. 
-Hello, Commando Cody.

170
00:07:14,882 --> 00:07:16,815
-What time is it? 
-Ted, Miss Gilbert.

171
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:18,507
How are you getting along 
with the rocket ship?

172
00:07:18,541 --> 00:07:20,336
-As well as can be expected, 
sir.

173
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:22,442
Our test with the model 
is satisfactory.

174
00:07:22,476 --> 00:07:24,236
If the government will let us 
make a few test flights

175
00:07:24,271 --> 00:07:26,135
with the rocket -- 
-It's out of the question.

176
00:07:26,169 --> 00:07:29,276
This whole project's 
strictly top secret.

177
00:07:29,310 --> 00:07:31,416
Since you people develop 
the rocket ship for us

178
00:07:31,451 --> 00:07:33,038
and we'll be the ones 
operating it,

179
00:07:33,073 --> 00:07:35,558
it has been decided that you 
should know more of our plans.

180
00:07:35,593 --> 00:07:37,491
-Gee, nice head, 
-I'm authorized to to tell you

181
00:07:37,526 --> 00:07:40,011
this much -- 
Its primary use

182
00:07:40,045 --> 00:07:42,703
will be to combat this wave 
of sabotage and destruction

183
00:07:42,738 --> 00:07:44,291
of our defense system.
-Duh.

184
00:07:44,325 --> 00:07:45,913
-You mean the government knows 
what's causing it?

185
00:07:45,948 --> 00:07:47,087
-Yeah, they read
the papers, too.

186
00:07:47,121 --> 00:07:48,398
-Tests of the wreckage show

187
00:07:48,433 --> 00:07:50,608
that the explosions 
are of atomic origin.

188
00:07:50,642 --> 00:07:53,127
-You mean someone's dropping 
atomic bombs on us?

189
00:07:53,162 --> 00:07:54,888
-Not bombs, no planes 
have been observed

190
00:07:54,922 --> 00:07:56,786
and the explosions 
aren't severe enough.

191
00:07:56,821 --> 00:07:58,788
-No. From what I've learned 
of the nature of the blast

192
00:07:58,823 --> 00:08:00,928
they seem more like 
an atomic ray of some kind.

193
00:08:00,963 --> 00:08:02,240
Of course, that's just a guess.

194
00:08:02,274 --> 00:08:04,414
-It's the same guess 
that we've made.

195
00:08:04,449 --> 00:08:06,624
Because it's the only 
possible answer.

196
00:08:06,658 --> 00:08:09,419
-But no one has ever been able 
to make an atomic ray machine.

197
00:08:09,454 --> 00:08:11,180
-You mean no one on Earth 
ever has.

198
00:08:11,214 --> 00:08:12,733
-Ooh? 
-Say, this rocket

199
00:08:12,768 --> 00:08:14,597
we're building will be able 
to fly to the Moon.

200
00:08:14,632 --> 00:08:16,081
Maybe someone up there 
has built one

201
00:08:16,116 --> 00:08:18,532
that could get down here. 
-We believe that possible.

202
00:08:18,567 --> 00:08:20,603
And for some time our 
astronomers have been noticing

203
00:08:20,638 --> 00:08:23,364
an unusual amount of atomic activity on the Moon.

204
00:08:23,399 --> 00:08:26,264
-Isn't any amount of atomic
activity on the Moon unusual?

205
00:08:26,298 --> 00:08:28,818
-Atomic activity on the Moon. 
Atomic blasts on the Earth.

206
00:08:28,853 --> 00:08:30,855
The two known facts 
fit together.

207
00:08:30,889 --> 00:08:33,685
-Yes, it adds up.
-Like a grocery bill.

208
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:34,928
-So that's why you want us 
to fly to the Moon.

209
00:08:34,962 --> 00:08:36,550
-Yes. And when you 
get to the Moon,

210
00:08:36,585 --> 00:08:39,001
find out if these attacks are actually coming from there.

211
00:08:39,035 --> 00:08:40,347
-And bring back some cheese. 
-We'll try to devise some

212
00:08:40,381 --> 00:08:42,038
plan for stopping them.

213
00:08:42,073 --> 00:08:45,559
In the meantime, we'll do all we can with these attacks here.

214
00:08:45,594 --> 00:08:47,112
-Is your flying suit 
still working?

215
00:08:47,147 --> 00:08:48,666
-No, it needs to be let out. 
-Yes.

216
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:50,806
-We may ask you to use it.

217
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:53,878
If a ray gun is being 
used to cause these blasts

218
00:08:53,912 --> 00:08:56,363
it's probably being operated 
from the ground.

219
00:08:56,397 --> 00:08:58,158
And if you could get 
to the scene of the attack

220
00:08:58,192 --> 00:09:00,263
soon enough, you might 
be able to find

221
00:09:00,298 --> 00:09:01,817
some clue to the attackers.

222
00:09:01,851 --> 00:09:03,163
-I'll be glad to try. 
-Good.

223
00:09:03,197 --> 00:09:04,716
-Or if we find out
what time it is.

224
00:09:04,751 --> 00:09:06,235
-I'll call you as soon as we 
get any information

225
00:09:06,269 --> 00:09:07,374
about another attack. 
-All right, sir.

226
00:09:07,408 --> 00:09:08,996
-Goodbye. 
-Goodbye.

227
00:09:09,031 --> 00:09:13,104
♪♪

228
00:09:13,138 --> 00:09:16,832
[Train whistle blows]

229
00:09:16,866 --> 00:09:19,835
♪♪

230
00:09:19,869 --> 00:09:22,044
-There she is. Right on time.

231
00:09:22,078 --> 00:09:23,286
-It's a train all right.

232
00:09:23,321 --> 00:09:25,703
♪♪

233
00:09:25,737 --> 00:09:27,049
-Whoa.

234
00:09:27,083 --> 00:09:29,810
♪♪

235
00:09:29,845 --> 00:09:32,261
Feels good, don't it? 
-That did it.

236
00:09:32,295 --> 00:09:34,056
-Nice shooting, Lionel.

237
00:09:34,090 --> 00:09:36,334
-That train will be along soon.-Thanks, Tyco.

238
00:09:39,164 --> 00:09:41,960
-All right. 
I'll get out there right away.

239
00:09:41,995 --> 00:09:43,272
Give me a hand with 
the flying suit, Ted.

240
00:09:43,306 --> 00:09:44,860
-Sure.
-You're gonna wear that tie

241
00:09:44,894 --> 00:09:46,206
to flying? 
-...just blasted out

242
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:47,759
in the west end of the valley.

243
00:09:47,794 --> 00:09:51,625
A ranch reported they saw 
a truck parked on a hill,

244
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:53,869
overlooking the tracks, 
there's two men in it.

245
00:09:53,903 --> 00:09:55,215
Working some kind of a gun.

246
00:09:55,249 --> 00:09:56,285
They pulled out 
right after the wreck

247
00:09:56,319 --> 00:09:58,080
and headed toward Kern Hills.

248
00:09:58,114 --> 00:10:00,047
Henderson's office gave me 
a description of the truck

249
00:10:00,082 --> 00:10:01,359
so I'm going off 
to look for it.

250
00:10:01,393 --> 00:10:02,843
-I suppose 
you'll be wanting this.

251
00:10:02,878 --> 00:10:04,914
-Yes, I'll take the gun too. 
-I may need it.

252
00:10:04,949 --> 00:10:10,092
♪♪

253
00:10:10,126 --> 00:10:13,060
-Good luck, pumpkin boy.
Take your protein pills

254
00:10:13,095 --> 00:10:14,717
and put your helmet on.

255
00:10:14,752 --> 00:10:16,098
-Window!
-Window!

256
00:10:16,132 --> 00:10:17,409
-Hello! Window!

257
00:10:17,444 --> 00:10:18,859
-Boy, that was close.

258
00:10:18,894 --> 00:10:22,414
♪♪

259
00:10:22,449 --> 00:10:23,864
-I'm outta here.

260
00:10:23,899 --> 00:10:27,834
-Oh. Oh, Mommy! Ooh!

261
00:10:27,868 --> 00:10:29,387
-Good afternoon, ladies
and gentlemen,

262
00:10:29,421 --> 00:10:31,251
we are cruising at approximately50 feet.

263
00:10:31,285 --> 00:10:34,047
The captain has turned off
the "no screaming" sign.

264
00:10:34,081 --> 00:10:36,014
And we just emptied
the washroom.

265
00:10:36,049 --> 00:10:43,435
♪♪

266
00:10:43,470 --> 00:10:44,989
-Looks like an atomic
nose-hair clipper.

267
00:10:45,023 --> 00:10:47,232
-Supposed to come along 
any time now.

268
00:10:47,267 --> 00:10:55,344
♪♪

269
00:10:55,378 --> 00:10:57,691
-Why do they hate trains
so much?

270
00:10:57,726 --> 00:10:59,520
-Ah, it's not so much
that they hate trains,

271
00:10:59,555 --> 00:11:01,315
they hate what they stand for.

272
00:11:01,350 --> 00:11:04,180
-Oh, that clears everything up. 
-Wait, what's that?

273
00:11:04,215 --> 00:11:07,701
[Train whistle blows]

274
00:11:07,736 --> 00:11:09,358
-Whoa.

275
00:11:09,392 --> 00:11:12,050
♪♪

276
00:11:12,085 --> 00:11:13,845
-Going down.

277
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:18,919
♪♪

278
00:11:18,954 --> 00:11:21,957
-Jane, stop this crazy thing!

279
00:11:22,992 --> 00:11:25,374
Oof.

280
00:11:25,408 --> 00:11:27,479
-I think he's got
to take a leak.

281
00:11:27,514 --> 00:11:31,656
♪♪

282
00:11:31,691 --> 00:11:33,762
-Oh.

283
00:11:33,796 --> 00:11:38,042
♪♪

284
00:11:38,076 --> 00:11:40,458
-Luckily they won't see him.

285
00:11:40,492 --> 00:11:42,460
-Now.

286
00:11:42,494 --> 00:11:45,290
-He was cleverly disguised
as a Hershey's Kiss.

287
00:11:45,325 --> 00:11:47,120
-I think he's just
drawing attention to himself.

288
00:11:47,154 --> 00:11:49,674
[Chuckles]

289
00:11:49,709 --> 00:11:52,263
-Why don't they shoot him
with the ray gun?

290
00:11:52,297 --> 00:11:54,852
-Well, because even back then
in the movies, uh,

291
00:11:54,886 --> 00:11:57,717
even the crooks had a sense
of fair play, Tom Servo.

292
00:11:57,751 --> 00:12:00,858
-Okay. Uh, it's a serial,
Joel, not a movie.

293
00:12:00,892 --> 00:12:02,411
-Right.
-A serial.

294
00:12:02,445 --> 00:12:04,033
-Let's get out of here.
-Stupid.

295
00:12:04,068 --> 00:12:05,863
-And leave the ray gun? 
-What else can we do?

296
00:12:05,897 --> 00:12:08,175
[Gunshots]-Well, you could take the truck.

297
00:12:08,210 --> 00:12:09,590
-Nah.

298
00:12:09,625 --> 00:12:11,385
-Much better to run in
the desert on foot

299
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:13,318
with no provisions or anything.

300
00:12:13,353 --> 00:12:16,597
Can eat your gun.
-[Chuckles]

301
00:12:16,632 --> 00:12:18,738
-Hey, it matches my helmet
and everything.

302
00:12:18,772 --> 00:12:21,085
All I need is a Spark Master
Playset and I'm done.

303
00:12:21,119 --> 00:12:23,673
-The loss of the ray gun 
is not too serious.

304
00:12:23,708 --> 00:12:25,261
I brought sufficient equipment 
from the Moon

305
00:12:25,296 --> 00:12:26,642
to build several of them,

306
00:12:26,676 --> 00:12:28,402
but I do not care 
to have the Earth people

307
00:12:28,437 --> 00:12:31,129
learn the secrets 
of our weapons.

308
00:12:31,164 --> 00:12:34,063
From the description you gave 
me of your attacker's costume,

309
00:12:34,098 --> 00:12:35,582
it must have been Commando Cody

310
00:12:35,616 --> 00:12:37,791
and his flying suit.
-Or Trash Can Head.

311
00:12:37,826 --> 00:12:39,310
-He will probably 
take our ray gun

312
00:12:39,344 --> 00:12:41,277
to his laboratory 
for examination.

313
00:12:41,312 --> 00:12:42,865
So you must go there at once

314
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:45,557
and recover the atomic chamber 
from the ray gun.

315
00:12:45,592 --> 00:12:47,767
-That's a large order, Krog.

316
00:12:47,801 --> 00:12:49,941
We were hired out to work 
your ray gun.

317
00:12:49,976 --> 00:12:51,701
This business of your -- 
-You were hired to do anything

318
00:12:51,736 --> 00:12:53,807
that I may consider necessary 
to pave the way

319
00:12:53,842 --> 00:12:56,154
for our invasion from the Moon.

320
00:12:56,189 --> 00:12:57,707
You're being well paid 
for your work,

321
00:12:57,742 --> 00:12:59,779
so do as you're ordered.

322
00:12:59,813 --> 00:13:02,540
-Yeah, well, paid,
but in Moon rocks.

323
00:13:02,574 --> 00:13:04,680
Maybe we can afford
one of your suits.

324
00:13:04,714 --> 00:13:11,376
♪♪

325
00:13:11,411 --> 00:13:14,552
-And this looks like
the right door.

326
00:13:14,586 --> 00:13:16,036
-Is working on this gun 
gonna hold up

327
00:13:16,071 --> 00:13:17,348
finishing the rocket ship?
-Maybe.

328
00:13:17,382 --> 00:13:19,626
-No. I'll just give it 
a quick examination

329
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:21,559
and then turn it over 
to the authorities.

330
00:13:21,593 --> 00:13:23,837
We'll still be able to take off for the Moon next Wednesday.

331
00:13:23,872 --> 00:13:29,049
♪♪

332
00:13:29,084 --> 00:13:33,260
-Oh, I hate to shoot
a butt like that.

333
00:13:33,295 --> 00:13:35,849
-Take it easy 
and you won't get hurt.

334
00:13:35,884 --> 00:13:37,333
Move back.

335
00:13:37,368 --> 00:13:39,957
-That's exactly what
my aerobics instructor said.

336
00:13:39,991 --> 00:13:42,476
-Grab it.

337
00:13:42,511 --> 00:13:44,375
[Glass shatters]

338
00:13:44,409 --> 00:13:47,171
-Yee-haw!

339
00:13:47,205 --> 00:13:50,312
-Good thing scientists
are such good fighters.

340
00:13:50,346 --> 00:13:53,039
-Yeah.
Scientist versus hired thugs.

341
00:13:53,073 --> 00:13:55,524
Geeks and thugs.
You gotta love it.

342
00:13:55,558 --> 00:14:03,532
♪♪

343
00:14:03,566 --> 00:14:05,706
-Boy, they're using
a lot of breakaway chairs.

344
00:14:05,741 --> 00:14:07,432
-Oh, I thought
that was a loose stool.

345
00:14:07,467 --> 00:14:13,680
♪♪

346
00:14:13,714 --> 00:14:15,164
-This is for Louis Pasteur.

347
00:14:15,199 --> 00:14:21,999
♪♪

348
00:14:22,033 --> 00:14:23,586
-They always fall.

349
00:14:25,554 --> 00:14:28,591
-Excuse me, may I cut in?

350
00:14:28,626 --> 00:14:30,904
-Another blow for science.

351
00:14:30,939 --> 00:14:36,530
♪♪

352
00:14:36,565 --> 00:14:37,946
1...

353
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:41,156
2...

354
00:14:41,190 --> 00:14:43,296
3 strikes, you're out of there.

355
00:14:43,330 --> 00:14:45,160
-Routine 27.

356
00:14:48,922 --> 00:14:50,924
-And don't try to follow us.

357
00:14:50,959 --> 00:14:53,444
-Tell your friends.

358
00:14:53,478 --> 00:14:55,377
-Hey, they left the ray gun.

359
00:14:55,411 --> 00:14:57,344
-Yeah, but they took
the batteries.

360
00:14:57,379 --> 00:15:02,211
♪♪

361
00:15:02,246 --> 00:15:04,420
-I didn't know Jackie Gleason
was in this.

362
00:15:04,455 --> 00:15:10,288
♪♪

363
00:15:10,323 --> 00:15:14,361
-They're in the middle
of a giant chess game.

364
00:15:16,674 --> 00:15:19,435
-Hm. Fresh ground pepper? 
-Calling Retik.

365
00:15:21,851 --> 00:15:23,681
-Looked like a pepper mill.
You see?

366
00:15:23,715 --> 00:15:24,958
-Yeah.
-Yeah, get it?

367
00:15:24,993 --> 00:15:27,478
-Mm-hmm.
-I'm laughing hard inside.

368
00:15:27,512 --> 00:15:29,066
-Come in, Krog.

369
00:15:29,100 --> 00:15:31,413
-A rocket ship is leaving here 
in five days

370
00:15:31,447 --> 00:15:33,104
to investigate our Moon.

371
00:15:33,139 --> 00:15:35,106
-I did not believe the Earth 
men possessed a ship

372
00:15:35,141 --> 00:15:36,866
capable of making the journey.

373
00:15:36,901 --> 00:15:38,489
-I just learned 
about it myself,

374
00:15:38,523 --> 00:15:40,491
but I'm sure 
my information is correct.

375
00:15:40,525 --> 00:15:42,872
-Stop talking into
a shoe buffer.

376
00:15:42,907 --> 00:15:44,012
-We will be prepared to take...

377
00:15:44,046 --> 00:15:46,497
-He looks like Willard Scott.

378
00:15:46,531 --> 00:15:49,500
-And don't forget the cheese.

379
00:15:49,534 --> 00:15:51,502
And speaking of cheese.

380
00:15:51,536 --> 00:15:54,781
♪♪

381
00:15:54,815 --> 00:15:56,817
Oh, how I love him.

382
00:15:56,852 --> 00:16:06,517
♪♪

383
00:16:06,551 --> 00:16:10,348
-They're gonna launch
from a miniature golf course?

384
00:16:10,383 --> 00:16:12,488
-It'll be fine if they clear
the windmill.

385
00:16:12,523 --> 00:16:19,323
♪♪

386
00:16:19,357 --> 00:16:21,739
-I still think this is 
no trip for a woman.

387
00:16:21,773 --> 00:16:23,913
-Now don't start that again. 
You'll be very glad

388
00:16:23,948 --> 00:16:25,881
to have someone along 
who can cook your meals.

389
00:16:25,915 --> 00:16:27,503
-I'll say we will. 
Don't give her any...

390
00:16:27,538 --> 00:16:29,126
-Yeah, and pretty soon
I'll be able to vote.

391
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:31,266
-I like to eat too.

392
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:33,923
-Nice ensemble anyway.

393
00:16:33,958 --> 00:16:36,305
-Are the dicks coming with us?
-Uh, no.

394
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:39,136
W-We call them
police officers now, dear.

395
00:16:39,170 --> 00:16:42,104
-Oh, I didn't know.
I can't even vote.

396
00:16:42,139 --> 00:16:44,727
-Well, the best of luck 
to all of you, sir.

397
00:16:44,762 --> 00:16:46,591
Hope this thing works 
all right.

398
00:16:46,626 --> 00:16:47,696
-So do we.

399
00:16:47,730 --> 00:16:51,148
Goodbye. 
-Goodbye, sir.

400
00:16:51,182 --> 00:16:55,773
-We'll just wait back here whereit's safe by the thrusters.

401
00:16:55,807 --> 00:16:57,257
-Hiya, folks. 
-Hello, Hank.

402
00:16:57,292 --> 00:16:58,707
All set?

403
00:16:58,741 --> 00:17:00,226
-All warmed up. 
We can take off right now.

404
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:01,986
That is if the ship will stand 
up the test we made

405
00:17:02,021 --> 00:17:03,470
with the model. 
-I think it will.

406
00:17:03,505 --> 00:17:04,713
Now let's go.

407
00:17:04,747 --> 00:17:10,753
♪♪

408
00:17:10,788 --> 00:17:16,794
♪♪

409
00:17:16,828 --> 00:17:19,003
Safety belts fastened?

410
00:17:19,038 --> 00:17:20,763
-They're sitting
in office chairs.

411
00:17:20,798 --> 00:17:23,076
-Fire power jets.

412
00:17:23,111 --> 00:17:25,458
-Make sure you have
your staples.

413
00:17:25,492 --> 00:17:27,184
-Lean back to simulate G-forces.

414
00:17:27,218 --> 00:17:28,771
-Fire main jets.
-Everyone.

415
00:17:28,806 --> 00:17:34,570
♪♪

416
00:17:34,605 --> 00:17:36,193
Good.

417
00:17:36,227 --> 00:17:38,885
♪♪

418
00:17:38,919 --> 00:17:41,267
In case of an emergency,

419
00:17:41,301 --> 00:17:43,407
your office chair becomes
a flotation device.

420
00:17:43,441 --> 00:17:50,690
♪♪

421
00:17:50,724 --> 00:17:57,973
♪♪

422
00:17:58,007 --> 00:18:00,700
-Why, you'd hardly know 
we were moving.

423
00:18:00,734 --> 00:18:03,392
-Well, believe it or not, we are, a lot of miles per minute.

424
00:18:03,427 --> 00:18:05,670
-She's living up 
to the test we made.

425
00:18:05,705 --> 00:18:06,982
Set a course for the Moon.

426
00:18:07,016 --> 00:18:08,156
-Right.

427
00:18:10,261 --> 00:18:11,814
-A lot of miles per minute.

428
00:18:11,849 --> 00:18:15,128
-Here, file this, I'm going
to the washroom.

429
00:18:15,163 --> 00:18:17,889
-Here sit in my seat.
I want to play with your stuff.

430
00:18:22,066 --> 00:18:23,619
-Hey, triple word score.

431
00:18:23,654 --> 00:18:26,726
Hey, is this a contraction?
You can't hyphenate.

432
00:18:29,349 --> 00:18:30,868
-You know, I can make it
in half the time

433
00:18:30,902 --> 00:18:32,663
if I had a straight edge.

434
00:18:35,286 --> 00:18:37,702
-Sure is sunny in space.

435
00:18:39,152 --> 00:18:41,913
-Just about here.

436
00:18:41,948 --> 00:18:43,743
-Halfway on the compass.

437
00:18:47,195 --> 00:18:49,093
-Looks like we're 
on the right track.

438
00:18:49,128 --> 00:18:51,130
-We ought to be down 
to another two hours.

439
00:18:51,164 --> 00:18:53,166
-Hope they have a HoJo's.

440
00:18:53,201 --> 00:19:01,416
♪♪

441
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:03,176
-There's a city just ahead.

442
00:19:03,211 --> 00:19:06,283
♪♪

443
00:19:06,317 --> 00:19:09,562
-Where do we set down?

444
00:19:09,596 --> 00:19:11,150
-Let's take her back 
in those hills

445
00:19:11,184 --> 00:19:13,911
where we can find a place 
to hide the ship.

446
00:19:13,945 --> 00:19:15,878
And I'll fly over the city 
in the rocket suit.

447
00:19:15,913 --> 00:19:17,363
-Okay.

448
00:19:17,397 --> 00:19:18,743
-Yeah, a guy in a leather jacket

449
00:19:18,778 --> 00:19:21,194
with his butt on fire,
they'll never notice that.

450
00:19:21,229 --> 00:19:22,333
-[Chuckles]

451
00:19:22,368 --> 00:19:24,956
♪♪

452
00:19:24,991 --> 00:19:26,303
-Set her down in that canyon.

453
00:19:26,337 --> 00:19:27,787
-Sir, can we land
the ship first?

454
00:19:27,821 --> 00:19:31,342
-Okay.

455
00:19:31,377 --> 00:19:34,897
-Man, the Moon looks
just like Arizona, you guys.

456
00:19:37,245 --> 00:19:39,661
-Does it really?

457
00:19:39,695 --> 00:19:42,181
-No.

458
00:19:42,215 --> 00:19:43,561
-You should know.

459
00:19:43,596 --> 00:19:48,773
♪♪

460
00:19:48,808 --> 00:19:51,707
[Metallic scraping]

461
00:19:51,742 --> 00:19:58,990
♪♪

462
00:19:59,025 --> 00:20:00,337
-Here, let me help
you down there.

463
00:20:00,371 --> 00:20:01,821
-Cut. Edit.

464
00:20:01,855 --> 00:20:04,858
-One small step for man,
one giant leap for --

465
00:20:04,893 --> 00:20:09,415
aiiiiieee!

466
00:20:09,449 --> 00:20:11,348
-Must go through a lot of pants.

467
00:20:11,382 --> 00:20:15,697
♪♪

468
00:20:15,731 --> 00:20:18,493
-Hey, it's either ancient
Greece or Vegas.

469
00:20:18,527 --> 00:20:20,736
Caesar's world.

470
00:20:22,876 --> 00:20:25,016
-Hup.

471
00:20:25,051 --> 00:20:27,329
-Well, this looks to be a wall.
I'll go this way.

472
00:20:29,573 --> 00:20:32,645
-You are going in the right direction, Earth man.

473
00:20:32,679 --> 00:20:34,129
-How'd they know my name? 
-Enter the first door

474
00:20:34,163 --> 00:20:37,615
you come to.

475
00:20:37,650 --> 00:20:39,272
Turn that control.
-It's Bob Barker.

476
00:20:39,307 --> 00:20:40,860
-The door will open.

477
00:20:40,894 --> 00:20:46,762
♪♪

478
00:20:46,797 --> 00:20:49,662
-Hm. Hot, cold. Open.

479
00:20:52,423 --> 00:20:53,873
-I think on the Moon,
they get --

480
00:20:53,907 --> 00:20:56,772
-[Whispering] Go on inside,
it's okay. Go on.

481
00:20:56,807 --> 00:21:00,431
♪♪

482
00:21:00,466 --> 00:21:02,191
[Rumbling]

483
00:21:02,226 --> 00:21:03,848
-Excuse me.

484
00:21:03,883 --> 00:21:06,782
-First floor, lingerie,
ready to wear, notions,

485
00:21:06,817 --> 00:21:08,267
toupees,
-Moon Man.

486
00:21:08,301 --> 00:21:09,647
-Three pays.

487
00:21:09,682 --> 00:21:11,787
-[Chuckles]

488
00:21:11,822 --> 00:21:18,380
♪♪

489
00:21:18,415 --> 00:21:19,968
-I am Orkon.

490
00:21:20,002 --> 00:21:23,212
This is my brother Xenon
and my other brother Xenon.

491
00:21:23,247 --> 00:21:25,284
-Welcome, Commando Cody.

492
00:21:25,318 --> 00:21:30,323
♪♪

493
00:21:30,358 --> 00:21:35,432
♪♪

494
00:21:35,466 --> 00:21:38,366
-I am Retik, ruler of the Moon.

495
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:40,160
-Apparently you were 
expecting me.

496
00:21:40,195 --> 00:21:42,542
-Of course, for many years 
our radio

497
00:21:42,577 --> 00:21:45,373
has kept us informed 
of events on Earth.

498
00:21:45,407 --> 00:21:48,721
And my men there have advised 
me of your every move.

499
00:21:48,755 --> 00:21:50,032
-I've loved you from afar. 
-I see you have detected

500
00:21:50,067 --> 00:21:52,587
our language. 
-Yes, all our people

501
00:21:52,621 --> 00:21:54,589
are required to speak English

502
00:21:54,623 --> 00:21:56,315
so we can operate 
more efficiently

503
00:21:56,349 --> 00:21:57,454
in your country

504
00:21:57,488 --> 00:21:58,765
-And wear underwear
on our heads.

505
00:21:58,800 --> 00:22:00,491
-Mind telling me why your men 
are carrying out

506
00:22:00,526 --> 00:22:02,355
that campaign 
of destruction on Earth?

507
00:22:02,390 --> 00:22:05,013
-It's an election year. 
-They are merely softening up

508
00:22:05,047 --> 00:22:08,327
your defenses 
for our impending invasion.

509
00:22:08,361 --> 00:22:10,881
-Why do you want 
to invade the Earth?

510
00:22:10,915 --> 00:22:13,573
-Because the atmosphere 
on the Moon has become so thin

511
