but I just couldn't accept

759
00:32:00,194 --> 00:32:01,229
the other opinions 
of my theory.

760
00:32:01,264 --> 00:32:02,748
-Have you ever been
squelched, Joel?

761
00:32:02,782 --> 00:32:05,958
-I was sure they could 
be proven.

762
00:32:05,993 --> 00:32:08,996
That night Flora offered to 
undergo a hypnotic test.

763
00:32:09,030 --> 00:32:11,722
-She became Fauna,
my little house plant.

764
00:32:11,757 --> 00:32:13,069
-Hey, it's Betsy Ross, you guys.

765
00:32:13,103 --> 00:32:15,416
-Assisted by her father...
-In a cameo.

766
00:32:15,450 --> 00:32:17,452
-...I hypnotized her.

767
00:32:17,487 --> 00:32:19,351
During her past life,

768
00:32:19,385 --> 00:32:22,181
Flora had lived among 
the great people of the Aztec.

769
00:32:22,216 --> 00:32:24,977
She told us such strange 
things, amazing things.

770
00:32:25,012 --> 00:32:26,599
-Naughty things. 
-Far back in those

771
00:32:26,634 --> 00:32:29,188
ancient years, 
her name had been Xochitl.

772
00:32:29,223 --> 00:32:31,225
She was deeply in love 
with a brave and high born

773
00:32:31,259 --> 00:32:34,090
warrior called for Popoca.

774
00:32:34,124 --> 00:32:37,127
-They had a mad posh for hats.

775
00:32:37,162 --> 00:32:38,818
-So they decided to run away

776
00:32:38,853 --> 00:32:41,821
even though it was her sacred duty to preserve her maidenhood

777
00:32:41,856 --> 00:32:44,065
and be sacrificed 
to the god Tezcatlipoca.

778
00:32:44,100 --> 00:32:45,687
-The god of decaffeinated
coffee.

779
00:32:45,722 --> 00:32:49,346
-But they were discovered 
by the tribal priests.

780
00:32:49,381 --> 00:32:51,452
-They prepared hearty soups
and broth

781
00:32:51,486 --> 00:32:53,005
and forced them on their guests.

782
00:32:53,040 --> 00:32:56,181
For they truly knew how to
handle a hungry man.

783
00:32:56,215 --> 00:32:58,321
-This is, uh,
chicken noodle soup

784
00:32:58,355 --> 00:33:00,737
that I let steep all day
in the sun,

785
00:33:00,771 --> 00:33:03,257
it's like, uh, sun tea.

786
00:33:03,291 --> 00:33:04,637
-Better than borscht.

787
00:33:04,672 --> 00:33:06,294
-Yep.
-For your good health.

788
00:33:06,329 --> 00:33:09,297
-See, Mommy's drinking it. Mmm.
Nom nom nom nom.

789
00:33:09,332 --> 00:33:10,919
Open up, here's
the steam shovel.

790
00:33:10,954 --> 00:33:13,853
Hot, hot.

791
00:33:13,888 --> 00:33:15,821
-Nothing like a hot
steam shovel.

792
00:33:15,855 --> 00:33:18,099
-Gets you goin' in
the mornin'.

793
00:33:18,134 --> 00:33:20,688
-As punishment, 
the warrior was buried alive

794
00:33:20,722 --> 00:33:23,035
and an eternal curse 
was placed upon him.

795
00:33:23,070 --> 00:33:24,726
-Then things got bad.

796
00:33:24,761 --> 00:33:28,558
-And then Zamfir, master
of the pan flute played.

797
00:33:28,592 --> 00:33:29,938
But that didn't last long.

798
00:33:29,973 --> 00:33:31,768
-As part of her punishment,
she was submitted

799
00:33:31,802 --> 00:33:33,770
to the dreaded binocular joke.

800
00:33:33,804 --> 00:33:36,290
-[Vocalizing]
-Then Ethel Merman

801
00:33:36,324 --> 00:33:38,464
opened with a song.

802
00:33:38,499 --> 00:33:39,879
-Oh, this is horrible.

803
00:33:39,914 --> 00:33:47,749
-[Singing in native language]

804
00:33:47,784 --> 00:33:54,825
-[Humming]

805
00:33:54,860 --> 00:33:57,380
-Kinda like fingers
on a chalkboard.

806
00:33:57,414 --> 00:34:02,178
-[Singing in native language]

807
00:34:02,212 --> 00:34:09,737
♪♪

808
00:34:09,771 --> 00:34:12,188
-Well, uh, that's better.

809
00:34:12,222 --> 00:34:14,431
-Peeling the chrome off my body.

810
00:34:14,466 --> 00:34:22,646
-[Vocalizing]

811
00:34:22,681 --> 00:34:25,132
[Drumming]

812
00:34:25,166 --> 00:34:28,169
-♪ That's a whole lot
of corn goodness ♪

813
00:34:28,204 --> 00:34:34,417
[Drumming continues]

814
00:34:34,451 --> 00:34:35,832
-Hey!

815
00:34:35,866 --> 00:34:38,662
-Hey, it's a Peruvian All State
representative.

816
00:34:41,251 --> 00:34:44,116
-He's stoned.
-[Laughs]

817
00:34:48,051 --> 00:34:50,778
-The ceremony lasted
well into the night.

818
00:34:50,812 --> 00:34:55,127
[Drumming]

819
00:34:55,162 --> 00:34:56,404
-What a party.

820
00:34:56,439 --> 00:35:00,615
-I've got rhythm in my diodes.

821
00:35:00,650 --> 00:35:02,617
-Let's see, now, what can
we do with her hair.

822
00:35:02,652 --> 00:35:04,378
Well, we could give her
a swingin' bob

823
00:35:04,412 --> 00:35:05,896
or -- oh, she's
scheduled to die.

824
00:35:05,931 --> 00:35:07,622
Just give her a blow
dry and a rinse.

825
00:35:07,657 --> 00:35:09,659
-It doesn't seem fair
that they tease her hair

826
00:35:09,693 --> 00:35:11,937
right before she's killed.

827
00:35:11,971 --> 00:35:13,732
-Teasing's always bad.

828
00:35:13,766 --> 00:35:16,562
-This has got to be one of themost boring rides at Disneyland.

829
00:35:16,597 --> 00:35:18,116
You realize that, you guys?

830
00:35:18,150 --> 00:35:20,048
-Aztecs of the Caribbean?

831
00:35:22,465 --> 00:35:24,674
-How low will she go?

832
00:35:24,708 --> 00:35:27,021
-Then she was bent at the waist.

833
00:35:27,055 --> 00:35:29,023
-Hey, watch your hand, buddy.

834
00:35:29,057 --> 00:35:31,439
-Kind of looks like "Dirty
Dancing," doesn't it?

835
00:35:31,474 --> 00:35:35,098
-♪ I've reached the end
of my life ♪

836
00:35:35,133 --> 00:35:38,791
♪ And I'm waiting for
the knife to fall ♪

837
00:35:41,587 --> 00:35:44,763
-Man, look, it's the Jets.

838
00:35:44,797 --> 00:35:49,940
-[Speaking native language]

839
00:35:49,975 --> 00:35:51,390
-What's he saying?

840
00:35:51,425 --> 00:35:52,943
-He's saying live fast,
die young,

841
00:35:52,978 --> 00:35:55,498
and leave a good-looking corpse.

842
00:35:55,532 --> 00:35:58,259
-That was years before
James Dean even existed.

843
00:35:58,294 --> 00:36:00,261
-Xochitl was adorned 
with the bracelet

844
00:36:00,296 --> 00:36:03,575
and the breastplate, which were engraved with hieroglyphics

845
00:36:03,609 --> 00:36:07,406
indicating where 
the Aztec treasure was hidden.

846
00:36:07,441 --> 00:36:09,201
Then they cut out her heart,

847
00:36:09,236 --> 00:36:11,272
but as she began 
to regain consciousness,

848
00:36:11,307 --> 00:36:12,860
in the exact moment 
I came to the end

849
00:36:12,894 --> 00:36:14,620
of my little experiment.

850
00:36:14,655 --> 00:36:17,968
She struggled 
and began to shout.

851
00:36:18,003 --> 00:36:21,248
-Aah! Aah!

852
00:36:21,282 --> 00:36:23,560
-Flora had a terrible attack 
of some kind

853
00:36:23,595 --> 00:36:25,459
and her blood pressure 
dropped to such an extent

854
00:36:25,493 --> 00:36:27,840
that we were afraid 
she might die.

855
00:36:27,875 --> 00:36:30,740
But a couple of weeks later 
we discovered that Dr. Krupp,

856
00:36:30,774 --> 00:36:32,776
a man who had suddenly become 
a dangerous criminal

857
00:36:32,811 --> 00:36:34,951
in the underworld 
had been there spying

858
00:36:34,985 --> 00:36:36,228
during my experiment.

859
00:36:36,263 --> 00:36:42,096
♪♪

860
00:36:42,130 --> 00:36:44,823
The experiment had been 
a complete success,

861
00:36:44,857 --> 00:36:46,894
but since we realized 
that no one would believe us

862
00:36:46,928 --> 00:36:49,103
unless we could come up 
with absolute proof...

863
00:36:49,137 --> 00:36:50,380
-We decided to search
in the basement.

864
00:36:50,415 --> 00:36:51,726
-...we decided to search 
for the breastplate.

865
00:36:51,761 --> 00:36:53,694
-I couldn't wait to show them
my bumper pool table.

866
00:36:53,728 --> 00:36:56,006
-Flora acted as our guide.
-And my fine selection

867
00:36:56,041 --> 00:36:58,699
of jams and jellies and compotesthat I had laid up.

868
00:36:58,733 --> 00:37:01,219
-We discovered a passage in 
the pyramid of Tlazolteotl.

869
00:37:01,253 --> 00:37:06,465
♪♪

870
00:37:06,500 --> 00:37:08,985
-Flora, our guide,
was tied to my waist

871
00:37:09,019 --> 00:37:11,436
so we wouldn't be separated.

872
00:37:17,407 --> 00:37:20,479
-Why Floyd brought the Beaver,
I don't know.

873
00:37:20,514 --> 00:37:25,104
♪♪

874
00:37:25,139 --> 00:37:27,383
-Oh, my, there's a light.

875
00:37:29,419 --> 00:37:33,458
-We're thinking of expanding
this whole thing.

876
00:37:33,492 --> 00:37:35,322
-Making a rumpus room.

877
00:37:35,356 --> 00:37:37,945
-For our rumpus.

878
00:37:37,979 --> 00:37:39,912
-And speaking of rumpuses,
there's Flora.

879
00:37:39,947 --> 00:37:45,401
-Right foot, left foot, right,
left, right.

880
00:37:45,435 --> 00:37:46,505
-Ah.

881
00:37:46,540 --> 00:37:50,060
-We'll have this
blocking down in no time.

882
00:37:50,095 --> 00:37:54,064
-Suddenly, there it was,
my six-sided bumper pool table.

883
00:37:54,099 --> 00:38:00,278
♪♪

884
00:38:00,312 --> 00:38:06,387
♪♪

885
00:38:06,422 --> 00:38:09,321
-I think a swag lamp here
and a beaded curtain

886
00:38:09,356 --> 00:38:12,393
would really brighten
the place up.

887
00:38:12,428 --> 00:38:16,535
-♪ Once a jolly swag lamp
waited by the billabong ♪

888
00:38:16,570 --> 00:38:24,060
♪♪

889
00:38:24,094 --> 00:38:25,717
-Go that way.

890
00:38:25,751 --> 00:38:31,378
♪♪

891
00:38:31,412 --> 00:38:33,587
-There was no denying it.

892
00:38:33,621 --> 00:38:36,106
We were walking in a circle.

893
00:38:36,141 --> 00:38:41,491
♪♪

894
00:38:41,526 --> 00:38:46,945
♪♪

895
00:38:46,979 --> 00:38:49,844
-Professor. I think we're up 
against a dead end.

896
00:38:49,879 --> 00:38:51,225
-Can't be possible.

897
00:38:51,259 --> 00:38:52,847
The Aztecs wouldn't build 
a secret passageway

898
00:38:52,882 --> 00:38:54,642
just to have an end up 
with no rhyme or reason.

899
00:38:54,677 --> 00:38:57,093
-Not the kind of Aztecs
you're used to dealing with.

900
00:38:59,751 --> 00:39:01,580
-It's a cul-de-sac.

901
00:39:01,615 --> 00:39:03,064
[Thumping]

902
00:39:03,099 --> 00:39:06,413
-Professor, 
it sounds hollow.

903
00:39:06,447 --> 00:39:08,760
-Well, stop knocking
on my head.

904
00:39:08,794 --> 00:39:09,899
-But it's so thick 
that it's senseless

905
00:39:09,933 --> 00:39:11,038
to try to break through.

906
00:39:11,072 --> 00:39:13,385
-My head? Or the wall?

907
00:39:17,113 --> 00:39:18,217
-I'll push.

908
00:39:18,252 --> 00:39:22,083
♪♪

909
00:39:22,118 --> 00:39:24,672
-Pinacate, lend me a hand, 
please.

910
00:39:24,707 --> 00:39:28,607
♪♪

911
00:39:28,642 --> 00:39:31,610
-Not that hand,
your clean hand.

912
00:39:31,645 --> 00:39:33,612
Oh, that wasyour clean hand. 
-[Grunting]

913
00:39:33,647 --> 00:39:36,822
-Helps if you grunt.

914
00:39:36,857 --> 00:39:38,099
-Flora, come on.

915
00:39:38,134 --> 00:39:39,480
-We jarred it open.

916
00:39:39,515 --> 00:39:45,037
♪♪

917
00:39:45,072 --> 00:39:48,075
-Go ahead, Beav.

918
00:39:48,109 --> 00:39:50,974
-It's the same set
we were on before.

919
00:39:51,009 --> 00:39:56,532
♪♪

920
00:39:56,566 --> 00:40:02,192
♪♪

921
00:40:02,227 --> 00:40:04,471
-It's the Phantom
of the opera.

922
00:40:08,406 --> 00:40:09,752
[Thuds]
-Ouch!

923
00:40:09,786 --> 00:40:11,961
-This must be a little 
room beneath the pyramid.

924
00:40:11,995 --> 00:40:13,100
-Good.

925
00:40:13,134 --> 00:40:16,414
-But I know it 
isn't the temple.

926
00:40:16,448 --> 00:40:18,381
-Look what I found! Come here!

927
00:40:18,416 --> 00:40:20,176
-A laundry chute.

928
00:40:20,210 --> 00:40:22,868
-What is it? 
-It looks like a well.

929
00:40:22,903 --> 00:40:25,077
-Well, I think it's an air 
shaft, but undoubtedly

930
00:40:25,112 --> 00:40:27,632
it will lead us somewhere. 
-Probably to the temple.

931
00:40:27,666 --> 00:40:28,909
-Maybe the air.

932
00:40:28,943 --> 00:40:30,980
-Well, then, what 
are we waiting for?

933
00:40:31,014 --> 00:40:32,602
-No doubt
it will advance the plot.

934
00:40:32,637 --> 00:40:34,466
-Come on.
-Thin as it is.

935
00:40:34,501 --> 00:40:35,709
-Women and children first.

936
00:40:35,743 --> 00:40:37,849
I'll stand here
and hold the flashlight.

937
00:40:39,816 --> 00:40:43,371
-Remarkable.
It was the neighbor's basement.

938
00:40:43,406 --> 00:40:46,685
Once again, I could use their
VHS without them knowing.

939
00:40:53,761 --> 00:40:56,108
We shoved everybody
through the little hole.

940
00:41:06,947 --> 00:41:10,053
-After descending 
several flights of stairs,

941
00:41:10,088 --> 00:41:12,539
we suddenly encountered 
the hair-raising face

942
00:41:12,573 --> 00:41:15,403
of the god, Tezcatlipoca.

943
00:41:15,438 --> 00:41:18,683
We had reached the lower temple and the Aztec tomb

944
00:41:18,717 --> 00:41:19,753
that Flora had mentioned.

945
00:41:19,787 --> 00:41:23,411
-And the lighting was good.

946
00:41:23,446 --> 00:41:25,034
You could shoot a scene here.

947
00:41:25,068 --> 00:41:30,384
♪♪

948
00:41:30,418 --> 00:41:32,317
Lights, please.

949
00:41:32,351 --> 00:41:34,284
-There we came across 
the skeleton

950
00:41:34,319 --> 00:41:37,460
of what had once been Xochitl.
-Buffalo chicken wings.

951
00:41:37,495 --> 00:41:38,841
-On her chest, 
the solid gold breastplate.

952
00:41:38,875 --> 00:41:40,118
-Xochitl.

953
00:41:40,152 --> 00:41:42,396
This is horrible 
to think I was she.

954
00:41:44,225 --> 00:41:45,537
-But you're you now.

955
00:41:45,572 --> 00:41:49,714
♪♪

956
00:41:49,748 --> 00:41:51,923
-You know, we're the first 
persons to break in here.

957
00:41:51,957 --> 00:41:53,476
-The other people use the door.

958
00:41:53,511 --> 00:41:56,548
-It's a world that slumbered 
all these years

959
00:41:56,583 --> 00:41:58,585
and begins to awaken now.

960
00:42:00,897 --> 00:42:02,761
We've found what 
we wanted so badly.

961
00:42:02,796 --> 00:42:06,109
-The sump pump. 
-No, Edward. Don't do it.

962
00:42:06,144 --> 00:42:08,560
-Do it, Edward, do it.

963
00:42:08,595 --> 00:42:14,117
♪♪

964
00:42:14,152 --> 00:42:16,534
-There's a Tootsie Pop
still in his mouth.

965
00:42:16,568 --> 00:42:19,778
-That's Kojak.
-They really last a long time.

966
00:42:19,813 --> 00:42:23,126
-The head should really
screw right off.

967
00:42:23,161 --> 00:42:25,439
-This'll look swell
in my office.

968
00:42:25,473 --> 00:42:35,138
♪♪

969
00:42:35,173 --> 00:42:37,175
-Now, at last, I'll be able 
to demonstrate this

970
00:42:37,209 --> 00:42:40,558
to my colleagues 
and prove my theory.

971
00:42:40,592 --> 00:42:43,664
- It's simply amazing.
-Simply.

972
00:42:43,699 --> 00:42:46,080
-Just a few days 
after I got the breastplate

973
00:42:46,115 --> 00:42:49,049
I asked you all here, including Dr. Krupp, as you'll recall.

974
00:42:49,083 --> 00:42:50,395
-Certainly.

975
00:42:50,429 --> 00:42:52,535
I'd say that it was 
the greatest experience

976
00:42:52,570 --> 00:42:53,985
of my life. 
-And mine too.

977
00:42:54,019 --> 00:42:55,607
-Me too. 
-You must also recall

978
00:42:55,642 --> 00:42:57,195
that I said that I'd study 
the breastplate

979
00:42:57,229 --> 00:42:58,990
and that I'd try to decipher 
the hieroglyphics

980
00:42:59,024 --> 00:42:59,956
and see what they meant.

981
00:42:59,991 --> 00:43:01,613
Since I knew that the message

982
00:43:01,648 --> 00:43:03,339
would tell how to find 
that hidden treasure.

983
00:43:03,373 --> 00:43:05,099
-Yes, but I remember 
your telling

984
00:43:05,134 --> 00:43:07,032
that you also needed 
the bracelet.

985
00:43:07,067 --> 00:43:08,240
-Exactly, Doctor.

986
00:43:08,275 --> 00:43:09,828
One object complemented 
the other.

987
00:43:09,863 --> 00:43:11,278
-Her ensemble. 
-Without the bracelet,

988
00:43:11,312 --> 00:43:13,694
it was simply impossible to 
decipher the code,

989
00:43:13,729 --> 00:43:15,627
so I decided to go back for it.

990
00:43:15,662 --> 00:43:18,699
In spite of the Aztec curse 
that surrounded it.

991
00:43:18,734 --> 00:43:21,391
-He who defiles 
the tomb of the Aztecs

992
00:43:21,426 --> 00:43:24,429
and finds the sacred plate 
will run the risk of death

993
00:43:24,463 --> 00:43:27,984
and his family as well until 
the breastplate is replaced.

994
00:43:28,019 --> 00:43:29,227
-And get big buckteeth.

995
00:43:29,261 --> 00:43:30,780
-What is he, Rod McEwan?

996
00:43:30,815 --> 00:43:33,438
-Lights. 
-My good friend Dr. Pinacate.

997
00:43:33,472 --> 00:43:35,613
So once again the three of us 
got together

998
00:43:35,647 --> 00:43:36,855
and returned to the tomb.

999
00:43:36,890 --> 00:43:38,339
-This time loaded out
of our minds.

1000
00:43:38,374 --> 00:43:39,720
-When we reached there 
and I was just about

1001
00:43:39,755 --> 00:43:41,273
to pick up the bracelet,

1002
00:43:41,308 --> 00:43:42,999
my father-in-law 
stumbled on something

1003
00:43:43,034 --> 00:43:45,312
and called my attention to it.
-Ow!

1004
00:43:45,346 --> 00:43:47,486
-Look here!
-I stumbled on something.

1005
00:43:47,521 --> 00:43:50,489
-It's my foot. It's attached
to the bottom of my ankle.

1006
00:43:50,524 --> 00:43:52,319
Boy, were we drunk.

1007
00:43:55,391 --> 00:43:57,255
-It's an Aztec IUD.

1008
00:43:57,289 --> 00:44:00,223
-Look here. Doesn't that grave 
belong to Popoca?

1009
00:44:00,258 --> 00:44:02,570
-Yes, according to 
